# 1. Executive Technical Overview

## 1.1 Architecture Philosophy

batmanoverlay is architected as a **layered, event-driven, modular monolith** — a single deployable unit composed of loosely coupled modules that communicate via Qt Signals/Slots. This approach is chosen over microservices (inappropriate for a desktop app), a plugin architecture (premature for v1.0), or a monolithic blob (unmaintainable).

The philosophy is:

1. **Layered isolation.** Each layer depends only on the layer beneath it. The UI layer never touches SQLite directly; the data access layer never emits UI signals.
2. **Event-driven decoupling.** Modules communicate through typed signals rather than direct method calls across boundaries. This enables independent testing, safe cross-thread communication, and future plugin hooks.
3. **Protocol-oriented interfaces.** Module boundaries are defined by Python `Protocol` classes (structural typing). Concrete implementations can be swapped for testing or future refactoring without changing callers.
4. **Data gravity.** Data models (Pydantic) are shared vocabulary. They flow downward through layers and upward through signals. No raw dictionaries cross module boundaries.
5. **Fail-safe defaults.** Every component initializes to a safe, functional state. Missing configuration files are regenerated from defaults. Corrupted databases fall back to empty state with user notification.

## 1.2 Design Principles

| Principle | Application |
|---|---|
| **Single Responsibility** | Each module owns one domain. `ClipboardManager` manages clipboard logic; `ClipboardStore` manages clipboard persistence. They are separate classes. |
| **Dependency Inversion** | High-level modules depend on abstractions (Protocols), not concrete implementations. `SearchEngine` depends on `SearchableSource` protocol, not `ClipboardStore` directly. |
| **Open/Closed** | Modules are open for extension (new signal subscribers, new search sources) and closed for modification (adding a new panel doesn't change `MainWindow` internals). |
| **Interface Segregation** | No module is forced to depend on methods it doesn't use. `WorkspaceManager` exposes a `WorkspaceProvider` protocol to `SessionManager`, not its full API. |
| **Least Astonishment** | API methods behave as their names suggest. `typing_engine.pause()` pauses; it does not raise if already paused (idempotent). |
| **Explicit Over Implicit** | Dependencies are constructor-injected, never imported as module-level singletons. Configuration is loaded explicitly, not cached globally. |

## 1.3 Engineering Goals

| Goal | Metric | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| Compile-time safety | mypy strict mode passes on 100% of source | Catch type errors before runtime; enable IDE support |
| Test coverage | ≥80% line coverage; ≥90% on core modules | Prevent regressions; enable refactoring confidence |
| Startup speed | < 5 s cold, < 500 ms warm | User perception of responsiveness |
| Memory efficiency | < 200 MB idle (0 tabs) | Coexist with IDEs and other heavy applications |
| Module independence | Any module can be unit-tested in isolation with mocked dependencies | Parallel development across team members |
| Zero-install deployment | Runs from a folder on any Windows 10+ machine without admin | Core portability requirement |

## 1.4 Architectural Constraints

| Constraint | Source | Impact |
|---|---|---|
| Python 3.11+ only | Technology decision (ADR-01) | All code uses 3.11+ syntax (match/case, tomllib, ExceptionGroups) |
| PySide6 (Qt 6) + Qt WebEngine | Technology decision (ADR-02) | All UI is Qt widgets; browser is QWebEngineView; styles are QSS |
| No admin privileges | Security requirement (PRD §NG-07) | Cannot use `kernel32.dll` calls requiring elevation; cannot write to `C:\Windows`, `C:\Program Files`, or `HKLM` registry |
| No network communication except user-initiated browser traffic | Security/Privacy requirement | No telemetry, no analytics, no background sync; update check is the sole exception (HTTPS GET, opt-in) |
| Portable data storage | PRD §FR-AC-07 | All data in `./data/` relative to executable; no AppData, no registry, no temp (except OS-mandated Chromium internals) |
| No process injection, hooking, or memory manipulation | Security requirement (PRD §15.2) | `SendInput` is the only cross-process API used; it is a standard Windows input API |
| Single-threaded UI | Qt requirement | All widget operations on the main thread; background work via QThread/QThreadPool with signal-based callbacks |

