# 5. Component Interaction

## 5.1 Allowed Dependencies (Directed)

```
Presentation → Application → Domain → Data Access → Platform
```

Within each layer, the following cross-module dependencies are allowed:

| From | To | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| ClipboardPanel | ClipboardManager | UI reads/writes clipboard data |
| TypingPanel | TypingEngine, TypingQueue | UI controls typing |
| BrowserPanel | TabManager, NavigationManager | UI renders browser |
| SettingsPanel | ConfigManager | UI reads/writes settings |
| SearchDialog | SearchEngine | UI displays search results |
| WorkspaceManager | BrowserProfileManager | Workspace switch changes browser profile |
| WorkspaceManager | DatabaseManager | Workspace creates/opens databases |
| SessionManager | WorkspaceManager | Session captures workspace state |
| SearchEngine | ClipboardManager, BookmarkManager, TabManager | Search indexes multiple sources |
| HotkeyManager | TypingEngine, ClipboardManager | Hotkey triggers actions |
| ConfigManager | ThemeManager | Config change triggers theme switch |

## 5.2 Forbidden Dependencies

| From | To | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| **Any module** | `ui/main_window` | No module may reference the main window directly; communicate via signals |
| `storage/*` | `core/*`, `browser/*`, `clipboard/*`, `typing/*` | Data layer must not know about business logic |
| `platform/*` | Anything above Platform layer | Platform wrappers are pure utilities |
| `models/*` | Anything except stdlib and Pydantic | Models are passive data containers |
| `browser/*` | `clipboard/*` | Browser does not depend on clipboard; communication is via signals through events |
| `browser/*` | `typing/*` | Browser does not depend on typing engine |
| `typing/*` | `clipboard/*` | Typing engine receives `TypingJob` objects; it doesn't know about clipboard |
| `typing/*` | `browser/*` | Typing engine is browser-agnostic |
| `clipboard/*` | `browser/*` | Clipboard receives text via signal; doesn't import browser |

## 5.3 Initialization Order

```
1.  Logger              ← First: all subsequent modules log
2.  ConfigManager       ← Second: all modules read config
3.  ThemeManager        ← Third: apply theme before any UI renders
4.  AccessibilityMgr    ← Fourth: configure a11y before UI
5.  DatabaseManager     ← Fifth: open databases for active workspace
6.  WorkspaceManager    ← Sixth: determine active workspace
7.  SessionManager      ← Seventh: check for crash, manage lock file
8.  BrowserProfileMgr   ← Eighth: create/load Chromium profile for active workspace
9.  ClipboardManager    ← Ninth: load clipboard data for active workspace
10. TypingEngine        ← Tenth: initialize (IDLE state)
11. HotkeyManager       ← Eleventh: register global hotkeys
12. SearchEngine        ← Twelfth: index data from clipboard, bookmarks
13. NotificationManager ← Thirteenth: ready to display notifications
14. UpdateManager       ← Fourteenth: check for updates (async, non-blocking)
15. PerformanceMonitor  ← Fifteenth: start metric collection
16. MainWindow          ← Last: construct and show UI
```

## 5.4 Shutdown Order (Reverse of Init)

```
1.  MainWindow          ← Hide window first (user perception)
2.  PerformanceMonitor  ← Stop metrics
3.  UpdateManager       ← Cancel pending checks
4.  HotkeyManager       ← Unregister all hotkeys
5.  TypingEngine        ← Cancel active typing; wait for worker thread
6.  SessionManager      ← Save final session; release lock
7.  SearchEngine        ← Close indexes
8.  ClipboardManager    ← Flush pending writes
9.  BrowserProfileMgr   ← Let Chromium flush cookies/cache
10. WorkspaceManager    ← Save workspace state
11. DatabaseManager     ← Close all connections
12. NotificationManager ← Dismiss toasts
13. ConfigManager       ← Flush pending config writes
14. ThemeManager        ← No-op
15. AccessibilityMgr    ← No-op
16. Logger              ← Log "Graceful shutdown completed"; flush; close sinks
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The typing engine worker thread MUST be joined (with a 5-second timeout) before proceeding with shutdown. If the timeout expires, the thread is abandoned (daemon thread) and a warning is logged.

