# 16. Security Architecture

## 16.1 Explicit Security Exclusion Confirmation

> [!CAUTION]
> This architecture does **NOT** include, and will **never** include:
> - Stealth mechanisms (window hiding from screen capture, process name spoofing)
> - Anti-detection capabilities (evading antivirus, proctoring, or anti-cheat systems)
> - Process impersonation (mimicking svchost.exe, explorer.exe, or any system process)
> - Screen capture bypass (WDA_EXCLUDEFROMCAPTURE or similar)
> - Security circumvention of any kind (browser DRM, application hooks, memory manipulation)
> - Privilege escalation (requesting admin, modifying UAC, token manipulation)
> - Code injection (DLL injection, process hollowing, API hooking)
>
> The application process is named `batmanoverlay.exe`. It always appears in Task Manager with its real name. Its window is always visible and capturable by screen recording software.

## 16.2 Defensive Security Measures

| Measure | Implementation |
|---|---|
| **Input validation** | All external input validated via Pydantic models with strict type checking |
| **URL sanitization** | Reject `javascript:` and `data:` URLs in address bar (Chromium also blocks these) |
| **Workspace name sanitization** | Regex whitelist: `[a-zA-Z0-9 _-]{1,64}`; prevents path traversal |
| **Import file validation** | ZIP structure check, size limits (100 MB max), schema validation before extraction |
| **Atomic file writes** | Write to `.tmp` → `os.replace()` prevents corruption |
| **No listening sockets** | Application does not open any TCP/UDP ports |
| **No IPC server** | No named pipes, no shared memory, no COM objects exposed |
| **Credential isolation** | Browser credentials managed entirely by Chromium (DPAPI-encrypted); app cannot read them |
| **Log sanitization** | Custom Loguru filter strips `text`, `password`, `token`, `cookie` fields |
| **Dependency pinning** | All dependencies pinned to exact versions in `requirements.txt` |
| **Dependency auditing** | `pip-audit` in CI; fail build on known CVEs |

