# 18. Testing Architecture

## 18.1 Testability by Design

| Design Decision | Testability Benefit |
|---|---|
| **Protocol-based interfaces** | Modules can be tested with mock implementations |
| **Constructor injection** | No global state; dependencies explicitly provided |
| **Signal-based communication** | Signals can be intercepted with `pytest-qt`'s `qtbot.waitSignal()` |
| **Separated business logic from UI** | ClipboardManager tested without ClipboardWidget |
| **Platform layer abstraction** | `InputSimulator` mocked to avoid actual `SendInput` calls in tests |
| **Pydantic models** | Models self-validate; test data construction is explicit |

## 18.2 Mocking Strategy

| External Dependency | Mock Approach | Library |
|---|---|---|
| Windows `SendInput` API | `InputSimulatorProtocol` mock | `unittest.mock.MagicMock` |
| Windows `RegisterHotKey` API | `HotkeyRegistryProtocol` mock | `unittest.mock.MagicMock` |
| Qt WebEngine | Not mocked; integration tests use `QWebEngineView` | Real Qt (pytest-qt) |
| SQLite | In-memory database (`:memory:`) for unit tests | Real SQLite |
| File system | `tmp_path` fixture (pytest built-in) | pytest |
| Network (update check) | `unittest.mock.patch` on `urllib.request.urlopen` | `unittest.mock` |

